      "commentary": "For his first boon — with all the powers of Death at his disposal — Nachiketa asks for the healing of his father. Let my father's anger toward me be gone; let him be at peace, and welcome me home when you release me. It is a stunningly generous first choice. The boy his father cursed to death does not ask for revenge, riches, or even his own safety; he asks that the man who sent him here be freed from anger and grief. This is the character the whole Upanishad rests upon: a heart already turned toward reconciliation rather than resentment. Before he seeks the highest truth, Nachiketa seeks peace between himself and his father. The order matters — the seeker who cannot forgive is not yet ready for what Death has to teach."
