      "commentary": "Nachiketa's thoughts turn upward, and he describes heaven as the realm where fear itself is absent — no old age, no hunger or thirst, no sorrow, and, most tellingly, \"you are not there, O Death.\" It is a vision of the deathless as freedom from the one shadow that darkens every earthly joy. But keep a careful eye on where this is heading. Heaven, as described here, is still a place — a better place, a place of endless pleasure without decay, but a place one reaches and could presumably leave. The Upanishad is letting Nachiketa (and us) articulate the highest thing the ordinary religious imagination reaches for: a paradise beyond fear. It sounds like the summit. It is not. The third boon will reveal that even heaven is not the final answer, and that what Nachiketa truly wants lies beyond every world one could go to."
